# ORDER MATTERS: _redirects is first-match-wins, so these specific rules must sit
# above the /members/:slug.html wildcard below. Otherwise the wildcard swallows
# them and rewrites to /members/<slug>, which no longer exists — a 301 into a 404.
#
# Removed from the site entirely (not just de-listed), so there is no alumni row
# to anchor to. These URLs resolve on the live site today, and §8 treats every
# currently-resolving URL as a hard requirement.
/members/gregory-quach.html  /team/  301
/members/neeraj-venna.html   /team/  301
/members/ramiz-hajj.html     /team/  301

# Jekyll served member pages at /members/<slug>.html and everything else at a
# trailing slash. Workers' default html_handling (auto-trailing-slash) already
# redirects the .html form, but with a 307 — which does not move Google's index.
# These rules pre-empt it with a real 301. _redirects is evaluated BEFORE
# html_handling, and :slug binds correctly against the .html suffix (verified).
/members/:slug.html  /members/:slug  301
/team/index.html     /team/          301

# --- Jekyll-era paths that must not 404 (§8, from the live sitemap) ----------
# The three template example posts. Lorem ipsum with invented authors, so they
# are not migrated (§4.5) — but they are in the live sitemap and therefore in
# Google's index, so they redirect rather than disappear.
/2019/01/07/example-post-1.html  /blog/  301
/2019/12/14/example-post-2.html  /blog/  301
/2020/07/02/example-post-3.html  /blog/  301

# CLAUDE.md is rendered and indexed as a public page by Jekyll — internal notes
# on a public site. Astro does not publish it; redirect the indexed URL home.
/CLAUDE.html  /  301

# §5b: "Join us" folds into the Team page rather than competing with Publications.
/join/  /team/#join  301

# The live /contact/ page is unmodified Greene template placeholder content —
# "Department of Metaphor", scrooge@mcduck.com, a phone number and a Google Maps
# pin in Nova Scotia. It is not migrated; the real contact route is the Team page.
/contact/  /team/#join  301

# @astrojs/sitemap emits sitemap-index.xml. The live site serves /sitemap.xml and
# §8 requires it keep resolving, so bridge the old name. Search engines follow a
# 301 on a sitemap without complaint.
/sitemap.xml  /sitemap-index.xml  301
